# ritzlib/drop.ritz - Drop trait for automatic cleanup
#
# The Drop trait enables automatic cleanup when values go out of scope.
# The rules are in docs/LANGUAGE_SPEC.md §13 (AGAST #1854).
#
# Usage:
#   trait Drop
#       fn drop(self: @&Self)
#
#   impl Drop for MyType
#       fn drop(self: @&MyType)
#           # cleanup code here
#
# A type that implements Drop, or that contains a field that does, is
# move-only: using it by value moves it, and the moved-from binding can't be
# used again. Every other type copies.
#
# The compiler calls drop() on each binding that still owns such a value:
# - at the end of its block
# - before return statements
# - on break/continue, for bindings declared inside the loop
# - before assigning a new value to the binding
#
# Drops run in reverse declaration order (LIFO). A moved-from binding is not
# dropped. Don't call drop() yourself.
#
# ritz0 (#2157) and ritz1 (#2158) don't implement all of this yet; see
# ritz0/test_ownership_free_count.py for each compiler's gaps.

# The Drop trait - types implementing this get automatic cleanup
# NOTE: kept as a comment. Both compilers now parse trait definitions, but an
# `impl Drop for T` doesn't need this declaration to be in scope.
# trait Drop
#     fn drop(self: @&Self)
